Anxiety in the Elderly
Geriatric & Frailty
Excessive uncontrollable worry, Prominent somatic complaints, Severe muscle tension, Positive Geriatric Anxiety Inventory

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-001
Reason for Visit: Acute severe somatic agitation and intractable panic symptoms threatening the ability to remain safely at home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an acute behavioral escalation with severe somatic symptoms, including palpitations and shortness of breath, requiring urgent medical assessment to exclude acute cardiopulmonary events and prevent physical deterioration or caregiver breakdown prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My chest is pounding, I can't catch my breath, and I know I am going to collapse if I am left alone."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms escalated progressively over the past 48 hours. The patient describes a six-month history of excessive, pervasive, and uncontrollable worry occurring more days than not, recently peaking in severity. Over the last two days, the patient experienced sudden unprovoked agitation, restlessness, and feeling keyed up. This progressed to severe muscle tension presenting as frequent neck and back pain, sleep disturbance with an inability to stay asleep, and being easily fatigued despite minimal physical exertion. Today, prominent somatic complaints such as palpitations, shortness of breath, and gastrointestinal distress have masked the psychological symptoms, alongside an excessive fear of falling or losing independence leading to complete social withdrawal and refusal to mobilize.
Functional Status: Bedbound acutely due to fear of falling; baseline independent with mobility aids.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver burnout, high risk of functional decline due to immobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/92 | HR: 108 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Appears tense, diaphoretic, and in moderate distress, clutching the bedsheets.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals sinus tachycardia with regular rhythm, no murmurs. Respiratory exam shows clear lungs bilaterally, rapid shallow breathing, no wheeze or crackles. Musculoskeletal exam demonstrates severe muscle tension in the cervical and lumbar paraspinal musculature. Mental status examination reveals excessive uncontrollable worry and hypervigilance. The patient exhibits prominent somatic complaints during the interview. Bedside cognitive testing is intact. Findings align with a positive Geriatric Anxiety Inventory profile.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is cluttered with medications disorganized on the bedside table; lighting is inadequate.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against physical pillboxes; adherence to baseline antihypertensives is inconsistent.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anxiety in the Elderly - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of exertional chest pain, radiation, or diaphoresis out of proportion to anxiety, with a normal baseline ECG previously documented and no ischemic changes on bedside rhythm strip.

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: SpO2 of 97% on room air, absence of pleuritic chest pain, and no unilateral lower extremity swelling or known deep vein thrombosis risk factors.

Hyperthyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of resting tremor, heat intolerance, or prominent ocular signs, though routine thyroid function tests remain indicated.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The clinical presentation of acute severe somatic agitation, marked by sinus tachycardia, tachypnea, and intractable worry, required immediate face-to-face assessment to differentiate a functional psychiatric exacerbation from a life-threatening cardiopulmonary event. Telephone triage could not safely exclude acute ischemia or respiratory compromise. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of missed organic pathology, profound functional decline, and immediate caregiver abandonment due to burnout. This urgent after-hours intervention successfully established clinical stability, ruled out immediate physiological emergencies, and instituted appropriate pharmacological and behavioral bridging therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of low-dose diazepam (2mg twice daily as needed for up to 3 days) for acute symptom control. Recommended comprehensive metabolic panel and thyroid function tests via routine GP pathology collection.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured hydration and breathing routine. Step 1: The patient engages in a 4-7-8 deep breathing exercise for three minutes when somatic symptoms begin. Step 2: The caregiver provides immediate positive reinforcement by sitting with the patient to watch a preferred television program. Cause-and-effect: The immediate application of an enjoyable shared activity directly rewards the utilization of self-soothing techniques, increasing the likelihood the patient will use breathing exercises during future panic episodes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral generated for a community mental health nursing assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a bedside commode to reduce nocturnal anxiety regarding bathroom ambulation.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 48-72 hours for ongoing management and pathology review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops crushing central chest pain, new-onset focal neurological deficits, loss of consciousness, or expresses explicit intent for self-harm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive pending formal laboratory investigations to exclude organic aetiologies; psychological conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute hyperarousal is expected to slowly resolve over the next 24 to 48 hours with compliance to the behavioral plan and short-term medication. Failure to address the somatic symptoms proactively will prolong the episode.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention immediately if you experience new chest pain that radiates to your arm or jaw, or if you find yourself entirely unable to catch your breath even when resting. Contact your regular GP within 48 hours if the feeling of panic does not reduce with the breathing exercises.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The short-term medication acts on the central nervous system to reduce the physical symptoms of panic, allowing the brain to break the cycle of fear and physical tension. It is a temporary measure while longer-term strategies are established.
Actionable Steps: Complete the 4-7-8 breathing exercise every time your heart rate increases. Ensure you claim your scheduled television time with your caregiver immediately after finishing the exercise to reward your effort.

